# Completion Checklist

Every implementation should satisfy:

✓ Business value delivered.

✓ Product behavior improved.

✓ Security preserved.

✓ Architecture respected.

✓ Data integrity protected.

✓ Performance appropriate.

✓ Reliability maintained.

✓ Maintainability improved.

✓ Operational quality preserved.

✓ Documentation updated when necessary.

Completion requires satisfying the complete system rather than isolated technical tasks.

---

# Incomplete Work

An implementation remains incomplete when:

Business ownership is unclear.

Architecture becomes inconsistent.

Security is weakened.

Data integrity is uncertain.

Technical debt increases without justification.

Important trade-offs remain undocumented.

The implementation depends upon undocumented assumptions.

Code that merely works is not necessarily complete.

---

# Constitutional Review

Before final approval ask:

Would this implementation still appear correct after one year?

Would another engineer understand it immediately?

Would the architecture become stronger because of this change?

Would users experience greater confidence?

If the answer is uncertain, further improvement is required.

---

# Final Principle

Done does not mean finished.

Done means the implementation has reached the quality expected by the Constitution.

Every completed feature should strengthen the product, simplify future development, and preserve the long-term integrity of hubIO.

---

# Article V — Handbook Dependency System

The Engineering Handbook is a unified system.

Individual handbooks should never be interpreted in isolation.

Each handbook defines a specific engineering concern.

Together they establish a complete engineering model for hubIO.

The purpose of this article is to define the relationships between engineering handbooks.

---

# Constitutional Hierarchy

Engineering authority flows from higher-order documents to lower-order documents.

The hierarchy is:

Engineering Constitution

↓

Project Standards

↓

Architecture

↓

Domain-Specific Engineering Handbooks

↓

Implementation Guides

↓

Generated Code

Lower-order documents must never contradict higher-order documents.

