# Article VI — Governance, Evolution & Amendments

Engineering is a continuously evolving discipline.

The Constitution is intended to remain stable.

Engineering Handbooks are expected to evolve.

This article defines how engineering standards may change while preserving consistency across the entire handbook.

Stability should exist at the constitutional level.

Flexibility should exist at the implementation level.

---

# Constitutional Stability

The Constitution establishes long-term engineering principles.

Constitutional principles should change rarely.

Changes require significant engineering justification.

Avoid modifying constitutional principles to satisfy temporary implementation concerns.

Long-term stability produces predictable engineering decisions.

---

# Handbook Evolution

Engineering Handbooks evolve through refinement.

Examples include:

Improved practices.

New technologies.

Better architectural patterns.

Operational lessons.

Business growth.

Every revision should strengthen the handbook without contradicting constitutional principles.

---

# Amendment Principles

Every amendment should satisfy the following conditions.

It should:

Improve clarity.

Reduce ambiguity.

Strengthen maintainability.

Improve consistency.

Increase engineering quality.

Support long-term product evolution.

Amendments should never increase unnecessary complexity.

---

# Reasons for Amendment

Appropriate reasons include:

New architectural knowledge.

Operational experience.

Measured performance improvements.

Security improvements.

Business evolution.

Platform evolution.

Improved engineering practices.

Inappropriate reasons include:

Personal preference.

Temporary implementation convenience.

Framework trends.

Short-term optimization.

Engineering governance should remain evidence-based.

---

# Backward Compatibility

When modifying engineering standards:

Prefer additive improvements.

Avoid unnecessary breaking guidance.

Existing implementations should remain valid whenever practical.

Evolution should reduce migration effort.

---

# Versioning

The Constitution should maintain explicit versioning.

Versioning communicates:

Engineering maturity.

Historical evolution.

Breaking changes.

Migration expectations.

Engineering documentation should remain traceable.

---

# Deprecation

Engineering guidance may become obsolete.

Deprecation should follow a predictable lifecycle.

Recommended process:

Introduce replacement.

↓

Recommend migration.

↓

Support both approaches temporarily.

↓

Remove obsolete guidance.

Engineering standards should evolve intentionally.

---

# Governance Principles

Engineering governance exists to preserve:

Consistency.

Predictability.

Quality.

Shared understanding.

Governance should enable engineering rather than restrict it.

Rules exist to improve decisions.

Not to eliminate engineering judgment.

